# Naming Conventions

## General

- PascalCase for components and React files that export a component (e.g. `App.jsx`, `UserCard.jsx`)
- camelCase for variables, functions, methods, hooks, props, and most JS modules
- kebab-case for CSS class names when writing new styles
- UPPERCASE for environment variables and true constants

## Patterns

- Event handlers: prefix with `handle` — `handleClick`, `handleSubmit`
- Booleans: prefix with `is`, `has`, `can`, or `should` — `isLoading`, `hasError`
- Custom hooks: prefix with `use` — `useHealthStatus`
- Express handlers may use `req` and `res`

## Clarity

- Prefer clear names over abbreviations (except common ones: `err`, `req`, `res`, `props`, `ref`, `id`, `url`)
- Avoid vague names like `data`, `item`, or `temp` when a better name exists
- Keep the same concept named the same way across FE and BE when possible
